# Where things come from

## Under the AGPL

`data/units.json` lists the parameters of each DSP unit and, more importantly,
the order they sit in. It is generated from
`src/hacktribe_editor/utils/ht_fx_ram_format.py` in

  https://github.com/bangcorrupt/hacktribe-editor

which is **AGPL-3.0**. Full text in `LICENSE`. The unit and field
names are that project's reverse engineering work, not ours.

The SysEx command numbers and the message header come from `scripts/e2sysex.py`
in https://github.com/bangcorrupt/hacktribe, also AGPL-3.0. No code was copied;
the codec here is a fresh implementation, and it differs from the reference on
purpose (see docs/geraetekarte.md, "Zwei Fallen").

The editor is licensed under the AGPL-3.0, see `LICENSE`. The standalone
Python emulator has the separate licence described below.

## From Electribe Studio

The pattern container layout (header size, block size, the single pattern
wrapper written by the device) and the rule to write only changed fields
over the original bytes come from Electribe Studio, the pattern and sample
editor by the same author. Its parser was the
starting point for `src/pattern.mjs`; field offsets are taken from the
device map instead of its constants.

## Fonts

The fonts in `site/design/fonts/` are IBM Plex Sans, IBM Plex Mono and Sora,
under the SIL Open Font License 1.1. The licence texts sit next to them.

## CodeMirror

`site/vendor/codemirror.mjs` is a prebuilt bundle of CodeMirror 6, the code editor of the Plugins
page, and of the small packages it needs (Lezer, style-mod, w3c-keyname, crelt, find-cluster-break),
all by Marijn Haverbeke and others under the MIT licence. The licence texts are in
`site/vendor/codemirror.LICENSE.txt`. The bundle is built from the npm packages by
`npm run build:codemirror` (`tools/build-codemirror.mjs`), so the page loads nothing from the
network. https://codemirror.net/

## Doom

`third_party/doom/` holds Doom for the card: `doom.bin` (the engine doomgeneric, GPL-2.0-or-later with
`sha1.c` GPL-3.0-or-later, linked with our adapter, AGPL-3.0-or-later, and with newlib and libgcc),
its complete source, and `doom.wad` (Freedoom 0.13.0, BSD). Each part keeps its own licence; the
texts, credits and the build recipe are in that folder and the reasoning is in `docs/doom-lizenz.md`.
The AGPL-3.0 of this repository covers our own code only. Not yet cleared for redistribution, see the
review in that folder.

## From Korg

Nothing. No firmware, no pattern, no sample. What the parameters mean is
summarised from Korg's public manuals for the same DSP units in its
workstations, mainly the M3 Parameter Guide; nothing is reproduced from them.

`*.VSB` is gitignored, and so is `.e2/`, which holds value ranges derived from
whichever firmware you point the tools at. Bring your own image, patched with
Hacktribe.

## Ours

Everything in `src/`, `bin/`, `test/`, `tools/` (except the emulator's licence below), `site/` (except `site/vendor/`, see above) and `data/` (except `data/units.json`, see above), including the design in `site/design/`. Every address, offset and rule was
measured on a Korg Electribe 2 Sampler, firmware 2.02, unless a comment says
otherwise.

## Optional Unicorn emulator

`tools/emu/runner.py` is an independent program under GPL-2.0-only, with
the full licence in `tools/emu/LICENSE`. Node test orchestration and the
editor remain AGPL-3.0-only. Node invokes the program by command line and
reads a JSON result; Unicorn is never loaded in the Node/browser process.

The tool requires the Python binding `unicorn==2.1.4`, installed separately
by the user. Unicorn Engine, by Nguyen Anh Quynh and contributors, is
published under GPLv2. Its Python binding identifies itself as BSD-licensed;
that metadata does not relicense the bundled native engine. Upstream source
and licence notices: https://github.com/unicorn-engine/unicorn/tree/2.1.4

No Unicorn code or binaries are redistributed in this repository or the
site build. If an installer later redistributes the engine, it must preserve
the upstream notices and satisfy GPLv2 source-distribution obligations.
The Python program's GPLv2 licence avoids assuming GPLv2-only and AGPLv3
are compatible within a linked program. No Korg data is included.
